        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.57,
            0.047,
            0.848,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Tale of Three Intersecting Lines"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, $\\angle \\mathrm{XAB} = 50^{\\circ}$ , and $\\angle \\mathrm{MAC} = 70^{\\circ}$ . Can we find $\\angle  BAC$ from this? We know that ∠XAB, ∠YAC and $\\angle  BAC$ together form $1 8 0 ^ { \\circ }$ . So"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.136,
            0.643,
            0.155
          ],
          "content": "\\angle XAB + \\angle YAC + \\angle BAC = 180^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.379,
            0.171,
            0.619,
            0.192
          ],
          "content": "50^{\\circ}+\\angle BAC+70^{\\circ}=180^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.401,
            0.207,
            0.598,
            0.226
          ],
          "content": "120^{\\circ}+\\angle BAC=180^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.409,
            0.243,
            0.589,
            0.262
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{Thus}, \\angle \\mathrm{BAC} = 60^{\\circ}",
          "image_source": "images/page_19_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.267,
            0.848,
            0.305
          ],
          "content": "Now construct a triangle (taking BC to be of any suitable length) and verify if this is indeed the case."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.312,
            0.293,
            0.337
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.345,
            0.85,
            0.384
          ],
          "content": "1. Find the third angle of a triangle (using a parallel line) when two of the angles are:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.206,
            0.399,
            0.336,
            0.419
          ],
          "content": "(a) $3 6 ^ { \\circ } , 7 2 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.423,
            0.345,
            0.443
          ],
          "content": "(b) $1 5 0 ^ { \\circ } , 1 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.446,
            0.335,
            0.467
          ],
          "content": "$9 0 ^ { \\circ } ,   3 0 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.47,
            0.334,
            0.491
          ],
          "content": "(d) $7 5 ^ { \\circ } , 4 5 ^ { \\circ }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.501,
            0.849,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "2. Can you construct a triangle all of whose angles are equal to $7 0 ^ { \\circ } ?$ If two of the angles are $7 0 ^ { \\circ }$ what would the third angle be? If all the angles in a triangle have to be equal, then what must its measure be? Explore and find out."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.581,
            0.63,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": "3. Here is a triangle in which we know $\\angle \\mathbf{B} = \\angle \\mathbf{C}$ and $\\angle A = 50^{\\circ}.$ Can you find ∠B and $\\angle C ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.827,
            0.575,
            0.93,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_16.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.386,
            0.626,
            0.578,
            0.78
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
